title: CaptiveCrunch CornFlake Process and ChocoShell CDP Artifacts
id: 67bb4c56-96fc-4828-997d-1f9b671587b4
status: experimental
description: >
    Detects process-level artifacts documented for the CaptiveCrunch
    campaign (Storm-2945 / Midnight Blizzard, active since May 2026):
    the CornFlake Go RAT running as svchost32.exe from a user-writable
    AppData path, and ChocoShell relaunching browsers with a remote
    debugging port to exfiltrate cookies over the Chrome DevTools
    Protocol, bypassing App-Bound Encryption.
references:
    - https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/07/31/captivecrunch-midnight-blizzard-targets-travelers-worldwide-for-malware-delivery-and-credential-theft/
    - https://malware.news/t/captivecrunch-midnight-blizzard-weaponizes-hotel-wi-fi-captive-portals-to-steal-microsoft-365-credentials/124711#post_1
author: Tia N. List
date: 2026/08/12
tags:
    - attack.t1669
    - attack.t1659
    - attack.t1557
    - attack.t1621
    - attack.t1548
    - attack.t1185
logsource:
    category: process_creation
    product: windows
detection:
    selection_svchost32:
        Image|endswith: '\svchost32.exe'
        Image|contains: '\AppData\'
    selection_remote_debugging:
        Image|endswith:
            - '\chrome.exe'
            - '\msedge.exe'
            - '\firefox.exe'
        CommandLine|contains: '--remote-debugging-port'
    condition: 1 of selection_*
falsepositives:
    - Legitimate browser automation (Selenium, Playwright) uses --remote-debugging-port
    - Application support binaries that borrow Windows service names; corroborate with network telemetry
level: high
---
title: CaptiveCrunch ChocoShell and CornFlake C2 Beaconing
id: 4ac775bb-cafe-42e3-8f7f-92d454dbdc11
status: experimental
description: >
    Detects network connections to infrastructure documented for the
    CaptiveCrunch campaign (Storm-2945 / Midnight Blizzard): the
    ChocoShell infostealer C2 address 213.145.86.112 and the CornFlake
    beacon domains ms365-device.com and ms365-live.com used to
    masquerade as Microsoft 365 endpoints.
references:
    - https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/07/31/captivecrunch-midnight-blizzard-targets-travelers-worldwide-for-malware-delivery-and-credential-theft/
    - https://malware.news/t/captivecrunch-midnight-blizzard-weaponizes-hotel-wi-fi-captive-portals-to-steal-microsoft-365-credentials/124711#post_1
author: Tia N. List
date: 2026/08/12
tags:
    - attack.t1669
    - attack.t1659
    - attack.t1557
    - attack.t1621
    - attack.t1548
    - attack.t1185
logsource:
    category: network_connection
    product: windows
detection:
    selection_c2_ip:
        DestinationIp: '213.145.86.112'
    selection_c2_domain:
        DestinationHostname|endswith:
            - 'ms365-device.com'
            - 'ms365-live.com'
    condition: 1 of selection_*
falsepositives:
    - None known; the IP and lookalike domains are unique to the campaign and are not legitimate Microsoft infrastructure
level: high
